<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sichelgaita</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1040–1090</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Sikelgaita, Sichelgaita of Salerno</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sichelgaita was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> princess, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guaimar-iv-of-salerno">Guaimar IV of Salerno</a>, who married the Norman leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-guiscard">Robert Guiscard</a> in 1058. She accompanied him on campaign, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anna-komnene">Anna Komnene</a> describes her riding armoured into battle at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durres">Dyrrhachium</a> in 1081. Her brother Gisulf II lost Salerno to her husband in 1077, and her son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roger-borsa">Roger Borsa</a> succeeded Robert as Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sichelgaita, also called Sigelgaita or Gaita, was a daughter of Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guaimar-iv-of-salerno">Guaimar IV of Salerno</a> and his wife Gemma. Her father had expanded the principality over large parts of Southern Italy until he and his brother were murdered by her four maternal uncles in 1052. His Norman allies avenged him and made her brother Gisulf II prince. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-guiscard">Robert Guiscard</a>, who had inherited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calabria">Calabria</a> from his brother Humphrey in 1057, needed a marriage alliance and divorced his first wife, Alberada, on grounds of consanguinity to marry her.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In the first years of the marriage her chief task was to bear heirs, and from 1065 she appears in charters beside Robert. Her <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> ancestry gave his rule legitimacy among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> who might otherwise have rebelled against a foreigner. When Robert was close to death in 1073 she made the vassals swear allegiance to her son Roger as heir over her stepson Bohemond. She tried to mediate between Robert and Gisulf, but Robert besieged Salerno in 1076; the city surrendered that December and the citadel held out until May 1077. Salerno became the couple's capital, and charters there twice title her duke.</p><p>In 1080 she conducted the siege of Trani, which ended with the surrender of the rebel Peter II of Trani, while Robert moved against Taranto. She is said to have argued against attacking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>, since Christians should not fight Christians, yet she joined the campaign. The Byzantine historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anna-komnene">Anna Comnena</a>'s picture of her rallying the troops with a spear is read by the historian Skinner as an attempt to belittle the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a>, while William of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a> wrote that she fled wounded by an arrow. Whether she fought is not known; the historian David Balfour finds no evidence that she was ever a combatant.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Robert died on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kefalonia">Kefalonia</a> on 17 July 1085 with Sichelgaita at his side. She secured his titles for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roger-borsa">Roger Borsa</a> and remained active in government, signing charters with her son, among them one placing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bari">Bari</a> under the city's archbishop. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orderic-vitalis">Orderic Vitalis</a> claimed she tried to poison Bohemond, a story John Julius Norwich considers unfounded. In 1086 she gave the town of Cetraro to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monte-cassino">Monte Cassino</a> in Robert's honour. She died on 16 April 1090 and was buried at Monte Cassino at her own request.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sikelgaita” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Robert Guiscard, the Norman conqueror who had married <b>Sikelgaita</b>, daughter of Guaimar IV, took Salerno from his brother-in-law Gisulf II, its last Lombard prince.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-13">13 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Robert Guiscard, who had married <b>Sichelgaita</b>, sister of Prince Gisulf II, besieged Salerno and took it in 1077, making it his capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-salerno">Principality of Salerno</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the Principality of Capua. Died in Cetraro, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.5,15.95&amp;year=1072" aria-label="Cetraro on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4944 749 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Sichelgaita"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5394 1019h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5394 1019h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=39.5,15.95&amp;year=1072">What was here in 1072? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
